.TH "tum\-pop" "1" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
\f[B]tum\-pop\f[R] \[em] attach the top of the push stack to the current
host
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tum pop\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]tum pop\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
Attach the highest\-push pane from the stack to the current host pane,
at the geometry recorded when it was pushed.
The pane is consumed from the stack \[em] its push field is cleared and
its host is set to the current pane.
.PP
The stack is \f[CR]list\-panes \-a\f[R] filtered to panes whose state
carries a \f[CR]push\f[R] field, sorted by push value descending, so
\f[B]tum pop\f[R] is LIFO: the last thing pushed is the first thing
popped.
Push several utilities from anywhere, then pop them one by one to
assemble a working set \[em] no layout file needed.
.PP
Pop is independent of pool/owned: \f[CR]home\f[R] is untouched.
If the stack is empty, pop does nothing.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R] Print a short synopsis (the SYNOPSIS
and EXAMPLES sections of this page) and exit.
For bare \f[B]tum\f[R], list every subcommand.
.SS EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tum pop                  # attach the most\-recently\-pushed pane here, at its geometry
# bound to  t P  in the shipped tum\-bindings.conf
.EE
.SS SEE ALSO
\f[B]tum\-push\f[R](1), \f[B]tum\-flip\f[R](1), \f[B]tmux\f[R](1)
